---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../site';

const sections: { id: string; title: string }[] = [
  { id: 'start', title: 'Getting started' },
  { id: 'addons', title: 'Add-ons' },
  { id: 'library', title: 'Your library' },
  { id: 'listening', title: 'Listening' },
  { id: 'problems', title: 'When something doesn’t work' },
];
---

<Base
  title={`Help · ${site.name}`}
  description={`Answers about ${site.name}: add-ons, your library, sync, downloads and what to do when something doesn’t work.`}
>
  <article class="container prose help">
    <h1>Help</h1>
    <p>
      Short answers about {site.name}. Something missing? Ask on{' '}
      <a href={`${site.repoUrl}/issues`}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>
    <nav aria-label="On this page">
      <ul class="toc">
        {sections.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.title}</a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <section id="start" aria-labelledby="start-title">
      <h2 id="start-title">Getting started</h2>

      <h3>How do I get {site.name}?</h3>
      <p>
        On Android, install the .apk from <a href="/#get">Get it</a> on the home page; the app
        checks for updates itself. Everywhere else there is the web app in your browser. The home
        page says what is out today.
      </p>

      <h3>What can I listen to straight away?</h3>
      <p>
        Public-domain audiobooks from LibriVox, podcasts from Podcast Index or any RSS feed, and
        your own files. {site.name} installs its own add-ons for the catalogs on first start.
      </p>

      <h3>Do I need an account?</h3>
      <p>
        No. Everything stays on your device. To continue on another device, pair it with a sync
        code.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="addons" aria-labelledby="addons-title">
      <h2 id="addons-title">Add-ons</h2>

      <h3>What is an add-on?</h3>
      <p>
        A small web service that answers the app: the catalogs you browse, the details of a book,
        and where its audio is. {site.name} plays, downloads and remembers; add-ons bring the
        library.
      </p>

      <h3>How do I add one?</h3>
      <p>
        In Settings → Add-ons, paste the add-on’s address, the one ending in{' '}
        <code>/manifest.json</code>. Some add-ons have a setup page of their own that gives you the
        address. To remove one, use its menu in the same list.
      </p>

      <h3 id="no-directory">Why is there no add-on directory?</h3>
      <p>
        An add-on is just a web address that anyone can build, host and share, and you decide whose
        you trust. We can’t check what someone else’s add-on connects to, or keep checking as it
        changes, so we won’t vouch for any of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why {site.name} lists only our own add-ons, and the SDK so anyone can build one. We
        don’t link to, review or promote add-ons made by others.
      </p>

      <h3>What does an add-on see?</h3>
      <p>
        What you ask it: the catalogs you open, what you search for and the books you look at. Audio
        comes from wherever the add-on points, straight to your device; it never passes through our
        servers. Add only add-ons you trust, and play only what you have the right to.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I build my own?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. The <a href="/docs/">add-on docs</a> cover the protocol and the SDK, and the{' '}
        <a href="/docs/manifest-builder">manifest builder</a> writes a manifest with you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="library" aria-labelledby="library-title">
      <h2 id="library-title">Your library</h2>

      <h3>Where is my library kept?</h3>
      <p>
        On your device: books, places, bookmarks, downloads and playlists. Add-ons can be slow,
        offline or gone tomorrow, and what you downloaded keeps playing.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I play my own files?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. On Android, choose a folder in Settings → Library. Or play from your own server in
        Settings → Servers: WebDAV, S3-compatible, Jellyfin, Emby, Plex, Navidrome or
        Audiobookshelf, and on Android also SMB and SFTP. The app talks to your server directly and
        keeps the passwords on your device.
      </p>

      <h3>How does sync work?</h3>
      <p>
        In Settings → Sync, show a pairing code on one device and join with it on the other. Your
        places and podcasts are encrypted on your device before they leave it, so the sync server
        can’t read them. You can <a href="/#selfhost">run your own sync server</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>How do I move to a new phone?</h3>
      <p>
        Use sync, or Settings → Backup → Export library on the old phone and Import library on the
        new one. The file holds your places, bookmarks, lists, tags, podcasts and add-on addresses;
        keep it private, as add-on addresses can hold your keys for them. Passwords for your servers
        and the sync key are not in it, so enter those again.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="listening" aria-labelledby="listening-title">
      <h2 id="listening-title">Listening</h2>

      <h3>How does the sleep timer work?</h3>
      <p>
        Tap Sleep in the player and choose minutes or the end of the chapter. It fades out over the
        last minute. On Android, shake the phone in the last five minutes for ten more. When you
        press Play again, smart rewind (Settings → Playback) goes back a few seconds, so you find
        your place.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I listen offline?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. Download a book or an episode and it plays without a connection. On Android, Settings →
        Downloads has the rules, such as Wi-Fi only.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="problems" aria-labelledby="problems-title">
      <h2 id="problems-title">When something doesn’t work</h2>

      <h3>An add-on did not answer</h3>
      <p>
        Add-ons run on their makers’ servers and can be slow or offline. Pull Home down to try
        again. Settings → Add-ons shows how each add-on’s requests went. If one of our own add-ons
        stays down, tell us on GitHub.
      </p>

      <h3>How do I report a bug?</h3>
      <p>
        Open an issue on <a href={`${site.repoUrl}/issues`}>GitHub</a> with what you did and what
        happened. Settings → Help → Diagnostics keeps a log once you turn it on: it holds no titles,
        file names, addresses or logins, and leaves your device only when you share the file.
      </p>

      <h3>Something infringes my rights</h3>
      <p>
        See <a href="/#rights">Report copyright infringement</a> on the home page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p>
      See also: <a href="/changelog">what changed in each release</a>,{' '}
      <a href="/privacy">privacy</a> and <a href="/terms">terms</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .toc {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .toc a {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 4px 12px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .help section {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
    padding-top: 24px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    scroll-margin-top: 96px;
  }

  .help h2 {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .help h3 {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    font-size: 18px;
  }

  .help h3 + p {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .help p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .help code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.88em;
  }
</style>
